// v3 Causal Kernel Specification
//
// The DAG is not just the IR. It is the closed physical world in which
// all conserved quantities live. Complexity, ownership, cardinality,
// and verification are not extra systems layered on top. They are
// different readouts of the same bounded causal object.
//
// This spec defines the causal kernel: which node kinds exist, what
// each one conserves, and how surface constructs lower into the kernel.

module v3.spec

// =========================================================================
// 1. THE CAUSAL KERNEL
// =========================================================================
//
// The kernel has three parts:
//
//   TOPOLOGY  — ports, edges, fork/join shape, bounded motifs
//   CARRIER   — type/cardinality/state space on each edge
//   LAW       — local monotonic facts: size effect, cost shape,
//               fan-out, consumption, boundary obligations
//
// Surface constructs (let, if, match, fn, lambda, fold, etc.) are
// sugar that lowers into a small family of bounded causal motifs.
// The compiler operates on the kernel, not on surface syntax.
//
// The test: can we add an arbitrary analysis lane (like complexity)
// by folding local laws over the kernel? If yes, the kernel is right.
// If we need heuristics, we missed a motif or a law.

// =========================================================================
// 2. CAUSAL MOTIFS
// =========================================================================
//
// Every expression in the language lowers to one of these motifs.
// There are no others. A motif is a node in the causal DAG with
// typed input ports, typed output ports, and local laws.
//
// The number of motifs is SMALL. v2 had 21 ExprData variants.
// The kernel has 5 motifs. The difference between a field access
// and a function call is DATA on the interaction motif, not a
// separate structural pattern.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2a. Constant — a leaf node with known bits
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// No inputs. One output. Cost = 0. No causal predecessors.
// Literals, data declarations, known values.
//
// Topology:  () → out
// Carrier:   out has a known type and a known value
// Laws:      cost = 0, fan-out = number of consumers of out

type Constant {
  id: NodeId
  value: Value                       // the known bits
  carrier: TypeShape                 // the type of those bits
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2b. Interaction — inputs transform to output via a known reaction
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The general-purpose motif. Covers:
//   function call, method call, field access, index, slice,
//   binary op, unary op, cast, record construction, list construction,
//   string interpolation, lambda application
//
// All of these are the same DAG shape: edges in, reaction occurs,
// edge out. They differ in WHAT reaction — that's data (ReactionKind),
// not structure.
//
// Topology:  (in_1, in_2, ..., in_n) → out
// Carrier:   each in_i has a type; out has a type determined by reaction
// Laws:
//   cost = reaction_cost(kind) + sum(cost(in_i))
//   size_effect = reaction_size_effect(kind)  // preserving, reducing, expanding
//   each in_i is consumed once by this interaction
//   fan-out of out = number of downstream consumers

type Interaction {
  id: NodeId
  kind: ReactionKind                 // what happens (data, not structure)
  inputs: List<Port>                 // causal predecessors
  output: Port                       // causal result
}

// ReactionKind is DATA. Adding a new kind of reaction does not change
// the compiler's traversal, cost model, ownership model, or emission
// pattern. It adds a value to this enum and a row to the reaction table.
type ReactionKind =
    // Navigation — accessing sub-structure
    FieldAccess { field: FieldRef }
  | IndexAccess
  | SliceAccess

    // Application — applying a known reaction
  | FunctionCall { target: NodeRef }
  | MethodCall { method: MethodRef }
  | BuiltinCall { builtin: BuiltinKind }

    // Arithmetic/logic — primitive operations
  | BinaryOp { op: BinOp, algebra: AlgebraRef? }
  | UnaryOp { op: UnaryOpKind }
  | TypeCast { target_type: TypeShape }

    // Construction — building structure from parts
  | RecordConstruct { type_ref: TypeRef }
  | ListConstruct
  | StringBuild                      // interpolation

// Every ReactionKind has an entry in the reaction table:
//
// | Kind | Cost | Size Effect | Inputs | Output Rule |
// |------|------|-------------|--------|-------------|
// | FieldAccess | O(1) | sub-value | 1 (receiver) | field type |
// | FunctionCall | cost(target) | from target sig | n (args) | return type |
// | BinaryOp | O(1) | preserving | 2 (left, right) | result type |
// | RecordConstruct | O(fields) | constructing | n (field values) | record type |
// | ... | ... | ... | ... | ... |
//
// The reaction table IS the spec. The compiler reads it. Adding a new
// reaction means adding a row, not a new match arm in every consumer.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2c. Fork — branch on input, both branches are real structure
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The input determines which of several subgraphs activates. Both
// subgraphs exist in the causal DAG. At runtime, one path fires.
// The compiler sees both paths (for cost: max, for ownership:
// exclusive consumers).
//
// Topology:  (scrutinee) → select → { branch_1, branch_2, ..., branch_n } → join → out
// Carrier:   scrutinee determines which branch; all branches produce same type
// Laws:
//   cost = cost(scrutinee) + max(cost(branch_i))
//   ownership: branches are exclusive — fan-out from scrutinee into
//     branches does NOT mean the value is consumed multiple times
//   exhaustiveness: all possible values of scrutinee must be covered
//
// NOTE: ExprIf is just Fork with a Bool scrutinee and 2 branches.
// ExprMatch is Fork with a general scrutinee and n branches.
// They are the SAME motif. v2 had them as separate variants.

type Fork {
  id: NodeId
  scrutinee: Port                    // the value being branched on
  branches: List<Branch>             // possible causal futures
  output: Port                       // joined result
}

type Branch {
  pattern: Pattern                   // what scrutinee value activates this branch
  bindings: List<Binding>            // values extracted by the pattern
  body: NodeRef                      // the subgraph for this branch
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2d. Chain — compressed repeated interaction with known bound
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A causal chain of N interactions, represented as (body, bound).
// The body is one link in the chain. The bound is the chain length.
// The compiler doesn't unroll — it reads the compressed structure.
//
// Topology:  (source, init) → [body × N] → out
// Carrier:   source provides elements; init provides accumulator;
//            body transforms (accumulator, element) → accumulator
// Laws:
//   cost = bound × cost(body)
//   bound is a structural fact: |collection| for fold, |tree| for descend, N for repeat
//   body's inputs are consumed once per iteration
//   accumulator threads through: fan-out = 1 per step (ownership: movable)
//
// fold, descend, repeat are all Chains with different bound sources:
//   fold:    bound = |collection|, element = collection[i]
//   descend: bound = |tree|, element = tree.children[i]
//   repeat:  bound = N, element = step index
//
// A "forever" loop is Chain with bound = Omega (system limit).
// Structurally identical to bound = 5. Just longer.

type Chain {
  id: NodeId
  source: Port                       // what provides elements/iterations
  init: Port                         // initial accumulator
  body: NodeRef                      // one link of the chain (a subgraph)
  bound: Bound                       // how many times the chain repeats
  output: Port                       // final accumulator value
}

type Bound =
    CollectionSize { collection: Port }   // |collection| — fold
  | TreeSize { tree: Port }               // |tree| — descend
  | Explicit { count: Port }              // N — repeat
  | SystemLimit                           // Omega — "forever"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2e. Binding — names a causal step for reference
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Not a computation — structural. It attaches a name to a node's
// output so other nodes can reference it. In the DAG, this is just
// an edge label.
//
// let x = f(y) → the interaction f(y) produces an output; the
// binding names that output "x" so downstream nodes can connect to it.
//
// Lambda is ALSO a binding: it names a subgraph (the function body)
// as a value that can be passed around and applied later. A lambda
// and a named function are the same motif — a subgraph with input
// ports and output ports, bound to a name (explicit for fn, implicit
// for lambda).
//
// Topology:  (value) → named_out
// Carrier:   value's type flows to named_out unchanged
// Laws:
//   cost = 0 (binding is structural, not computational)
//   fan-out of named_out = number of references to this binding

type Binding {
  id: NodeId
  name: BindingId                    // how other nodes reference this
  value: Port                        // what this binding names
  scope: NodeRef?                    // body where this binding is visible
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2f. Subgraph — a reusable causal structure (functions, lambdas)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A subgraph is a bounded piece of the causal DAG with input ports
// (parameters) and an output port (return value). Functions and
// lambdas are the SAME motif — both are subgraphs. The difference
// is surface syntax only:
//   fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int { a + b }     → named subgraph
//   x => x + 1                                   → anonymous subgraph
//
// The compiler treats them identically. Cost, ownership, emission
// all see the same structure.
//
// Topology:  (param_1, ..., param_n) → body_nodes → out
// Carrier:   params have declared types; out has return type
// Laws:
//   cost = cost(body)
//   calling this subgraph = Interaction with kind = FunctionCall

type Subgraph {
  id: NodeId
  params: List<ParamPort>            // input ports with types
  body: NodeRef                      // the causal structure
  output: Port                       // return value
}

type ParamPort {
  id: BindingId
  carrier: TypeShape                 // parameter type
}

// =========================================================================
// 3. PORTS AND EDGES
// =========================================================================
//
// Ports are the connection points. Edges connect output ports to
// input ports. Every edge carries a typed value forward in time.
// No back-edges. No cycles. The DAG is causal.

type Port {
  id: PortId
  carrier: TypeShape                 // what type flows through this port
  provenance: Provenance             // where this value came from
}

// Provenance tracks the causal history of a value. Not reconstructed
// downstream — computed when the port is created.
type Provenance =
    Source                           // original value (literal, parameter)
  | SubValue { parent: PortId, relation: SubValueRelation }  // derived from parent
  | Computed { reaction: NodeId }    // result of an interaction
  | Branched { fork: NodeId }        // result of a fork (one of several branches)
  | Accumulated { chain: NodeId }    // result of a chain (threaded accumulator)

// =========================================================================
// 4. LOCAL LAWS (conserved quantities)
// =========================================================================
//
// Every motif has local laws — facts that are determined by the
// motif's structure and can be read without global analysis.
//
// Complexity, ownership, effects are FOLDS of local laws over the
// kernel. The fold is uniform. The laws are per-motif. Adding a new
// analysis = defining a new fold over the same laws.

// Cost law: what does this motif cost?
fn motif_cost(node: KernelNode) -> CostExpr {
  match node {
    Constant { .. }     => O_1                        // free
    Interaction { kind, inputs, .. } =>
      reaction_cost(kind) + sum(inputs |> map(cost))  // reaction + inputs
    Fork { scrutinee, branches, .. } =>
      cost(scrutinee) + max(branches |> map(b => cost(b.body)))  // max branch
    Chain { body, bound, .. } =>
      bound_value(bound) * cost(body)                 // bound × body
    Binding { .. }      => O_1                        // structural, free
    Subgraph { body, .. } => cost(body)               // body cost
  }
}

// Size law: what happens to the size of the input?
fn motif_size_effect(node: KernelNode) -> SizeEffect {
  match node {
    Constant { .. }     => Constructing               // creates new value
    Interaction { kind, .. } => reaction_size_effect(kind)  // from table
    Fork { .. }         => Preserving                 // output ≤ input
    Chain { .. }        => BoundedBy { bound: bound } // output bounded by chain length
    Binding { .. }      => Preserving                 // pass-through
    Subgraph { .. }     => body_size_effect            // from body
  }
}

// Ownership law: how are inputs consumed?
fn motif_consumption(node: KernelNode) -> List<ConsumptionFact> {
  match node {
    Constant { .. }     => []                         // no inputs
    Interaction { inputs, .. } =>
      inputs |> map(i => Consumed { port: i })        // each input consumed once
    Fork { scrutinee, branches, .. } =>
      [Inspected { port: scrutinee }]                 // scrutinee inspected, not consumed
      ++ branches |> flat_map(b => consumption(b.body))  // branches exclusive
    Chain { source, init, body, .. } =>
      [Iterated { port: source },                     // source iterated
       Threaded { port: init }]                        // accumulator threaded (movable)
    Binding { value, .. } =>
      [PassThrough { port: value }]                   // naming, not consuming
    Subgraph { .. } => []                             // deferred, not executed here
  }
}

// =========================================================================
// 5. SURFACE LOWERING
// =========================================================================
//
// Every surface construct lowers to kernel motifs. This is where the
// 21 ExprData variants collapse. The lowering is NOT interpretation —
// it's structural translation. The surface syntax is sugar; the kernel
// is the physical reality.

// let x = f(y) in body
//   → Binding { name: x, value: Interaction { kind: FunctionCall, inputs: [y] }, scope: body }

// if P then A else B
//   → Fork { scrutinee: P, branches: [{pattern: True, body: A}, {pattern: False, body: B}] }

// match x { A => a, B => b }
//   → Fork { scrutinee: x, branches: [{pattern: A, body: a}, {pattern: B, body: b}] }

// items |> fold(init: z, fn: f)
//   → Chain { source: items, init: z, body: f, bound: CollectionSize { collection: items } }

// items |> map(f)
//   → Chain { source: items, init: [], body: append(acc, f(elem)), bound: CollectionSize { collection: items } }

// items |> filter(f)
//   → Chain { source: items, init: [], body: if f(elem) then append(acc, elem) else acc, bound: CollectionSize { collection: items } }

// x.field
//   → Interaction { kind: FieldAccess { field }, inputs: [x] }

// f(x, y)
//   → Interaction { kind: FunctionCall { target: f }, inputs: [x, y] }

// x + y
//   → Interaction { kind: BinaryOp { op: Add }, inputs: [x, y] }

// fn add(a, b) { a + b }
//   → Subgraph { params: [a, b], body: Interaction { kind: BinaryOp, inputs: [a, b] } }

// x => x + 1
//   → Subgraph { params: [x], body: Interaction { kind: BinaryOp, inputs: [x, 1] } }
//   (SAME motif as named fn — lambda desugars to anonymous Subgraph)

// Record { a: 1, b: 2 }
//   → Interaction { kind: RecordConstruct { type: Record }, inputs: [1, 2] }

// [1, 2, 3]
//   → Interaction { kind: ListConstruct, inputs: [1, 2, 3] }

// =========================================================================
// 6. READINGS (not passes — folds over the kernel)
// =========================================================================
//
// Complexity = fold of cost laws over the kernel
// Ownership = reading of fan-out + consumption facts from the graph
// Effects = fold of effect laws over the kernel
// Termination = structural fact: the DAG is acyclic + all chains bounded
//
// None of these are "passes." They are readings of the causal structure.
// Adding a new reading means defining a new fold over the same local
// laws. No heuristics. No reconstruction. The structure already has
// the information.

// =========================================================================
// 7. EMISSION (translation of kernel to target syntax)
// =========================================================================
//
// Emission is translation: (kernel motif, LanguageSpec) → text.
// The emitter has one case per motif (5), not one case per surface
// construct (21). The target-specific variation is in LanguageSpec
// (data), not in the emitter (code).
//
// | Motif | Rust | Python | Go |
// |-------|------|--------|-----|
// | Constant | `const`/`static` | module-level | `var` |
// | Interaction | `callee(args)` | `callee(args)` | `callee(args)` |
// | Fork | `match` | `match` | `switch` |
// | Chain | `iter().fold()` | `functools.reduce` | `for range` |
// | Binding | `let x = ...` | `x = ...` | `x := ...` |
// | Subgraph | `fn`/closure | `def`/`lambda` | `func` |
//
// Differences between targets (Rc vs GC, &T vs pointer, async syntax)
// are LanguageSpec data that parameterize the translation, not separate
// emitter code paths.

// =========================================================================
// 8. HARD RULES
// =========================================================================
//
// MUST:
//   - every surface construct lowers to a kernel motif
//   - every motif has local laws for cost, size, and consumption
//   - every analysis is a fold over local laws (no global heuristics)
//   - every emission is a translation of motifs (no semantic decisions)
//   - every new reaction is a row in the reaction table, not a new motif
//
// MUST NOT:
//   - add a new motif without proving existing motifs can't express it
//   - add an analysis that walks the graph and reconstructs structure
//   - add an emission path with target-specific semantic decisions
//   - treat lambdas differently from named functions
//   - treat if differently from match

// =========================================================================
// 9. ACCEPTANCE TESTS
// =========================================================================
//
// T1: New reaction test
//     Adding a new builtin operation requires: one ReactionKind value,
//     one row in the reaction table. Zero changes to complexity,
//     ownership, or emission code.
//
// T2: New analysis test
//     Adding a new analysis lane (e.g., purity, security classification)
//     requires: defining a fold over local laws. Zero heuristics. Zero
//     reconstruction.
//
// T3: Lambda = function test
//     All tests pass when lambda syntax is replaced with equivalent
//     named function + reference. The kernel does not distinguish them.
//
// T4: Fork uniformity test
//     All tests pass when if/else is replaced with equivalent match on
//     Bool. The kernel does not distinguish them.
//
// T5: Cost accounting test
//     For any subgraph, total_cost = fold of motif_cost over all nodes.
//     No separate complexity pass needed. The result matches the current
//     CX analyzer (where it doesn't have violations).
//
// T6: Ownership accounting test
//     For any binding, fan_out = count of edges from that port.
//     last_use = edge with latest topological position.
//     No separate ownership pass needed.
//
// T7: No ungrounded control
//     Every branch lowers to Fork with explicit join.
//     Every loop lowers to Chain with explicit bound.
//     Every recursion lowers to Chain (descent over tree structure).
//     No unbounded control flow is representable.

// =========================================================================
// 10. V2 EMBRYOS
// =========================================================================
//
// v2 already has pieces of this kernel, partially realized:
//
// - Node.children IS causal structure (topology)
// - ExprData IS the motif tag (but 21 variants instead of 5)
// - expr_child_roles / wrapper_child_roles = child layout as data
//   (the reaction table, but hardcoded as a map instead of structural)
// - node_field_roles = which fields carry causal structure vs metadata
//   (topology vs carrier distinction)
// - function_size_effects = local monotonic laws
//   (size law, but hardcoded instead of structural)
// - ExprCall.descent_evidence = provenance on interactions
//   (carrier law, but only on one motif instead of all)
// - MethodSemantics.size_effect, cost_shape = laws on reactions
//   (reaction table entries, but only for methods)
// - map_children() = generic kernel walker
//   (fold over topology, but only for one level)
//
// The gap: these are scattered, incomplete, and not unified. The kernel
// makes them ONE system: motifs + laws + fold. v2's 21 variants, 33
// heuristics, and 665 match arms are the cost of not having the kernel.
